\(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) @NMOFs-to-bimetallic CuFe PBA transformation: An instant catalyst with oxidase-mimicking activity for highly sensitive impedimetric biosensor
National Engineering Research Center for Carbohydrate Synthesis, Key Lab of Fluorine and Silicon for Energy Materials and Chemistry of Ministry of Education, Key Laboratory for Green Chemistry of Jiangxi Province, Department of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Jiangxi Normal University, Nanchang, 330022, PR China
## A R T I C L E  I N F O
## A B S T R A C T
Keywords: CuFe PBA 3D DNA walker Instant catalyst 4-CN miRNA-10b
In this work, a facile impedance biosensor was constructed for sensitive assaying of miRNA-10b based on the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) modified \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-metal organic frameworks \((\mathrm{NMOF}@\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})\) coupling with a three-dimensional (3D) DNA walker signal amplification strategy. Specifically, abundant \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) can adhere to the MOF via the coordination reaction between \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), which can be applied as a skeleton to produce CuFe Prussian blue analogue@NMOF (CuFe PBA@NMOF) just in time. Meanwhile, the carboxyl group, which is rich in the organic ligands of the NMOF, can be used to assemble DNA strands (complementary strand, CS) \((\mathrm{CS-NMOF}@\mathrm{Cu}^{2+})\) for biorecognition reaction. With the introduction of the target, a 3D DNA walker was triggered to shear out large amounts of assistant strands (AS), which were then anchored on the surface of GCE. Afterward, CS-NMOF@Cu2+ can be assembled on the GCE by hybridization with AS. Eventually, abundant CuFe PBA@NMOF were generated in situ on the electrode with the help of \(\mathrm{K}_{3}(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6})\), which can catalyze the 4-chloro-1-naphthol (4-CN) precipitation without \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\), thereby increasing the resistance of the platform. Under the optimal conditions, the EIS biosensor presents reliable analytical performance in a wide linear range from \(0.8~\mathrm{pM}\) to \(250~\mathrm{pM}\) with a low detection limit of \(0.5~\mathrm{pM}\).
## 1. Introduction
Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma (PDAC) is one of the deadliest cancers in the world, with a survival rate of only \(7\%\) of patients (Siegel et al., 2014). Unfortunately, there is no obviously symptoms presented in the early stages of the patients with PDAC, which greatly hinders early detection and diagnosis of cancer, resulting in an increase in patient mortality each year (Dhayat et al., 2018). Therefore, it is an urgent need for accurate detection of PDAC at early or pre-cancerous stages. MicroRNAs (miRNAs) are a class of short single-stranded RNAs of approximately 19–25 nucleotides in length, encoded by endogenous genes (He and Hannon, 2004). It can be used as a marker for the early diagnosis of tumors in plasma or serum (Mao et al., 2019). Previous studies have shown that miRNA-10b is overexpressed in cancer cells with PDAC and that this overexpression is associated with decreased survival in patients (Preis et al., 2011). Thus, miRNA-10b in plasma can be used as a critical biomarker for the diagnosis of PDAC (Cote et al., 2014). It is urgent to develop highly sensitive miRNA detection methods.
Recently, several analytical methods combined with biochemical analysis have emerged, which have been widely used for rapid quantification of miRNA, including surface plasmon resonance bioanalysis (He et al., 2017), electrochemiluminescence bioanalysis (Liao et al., 2019), fluorescence bioanalysis (Xu et al., 2019b), and electrochemical bioanalysis (Gai et al., 2018; Tian et al., 2018). The electrochemical bioanalysis method has the advantages of low cost, high sensitivity, and good biocompatibility. Therefore, this ultra-sensitive technique can be widely used for the microanalysis of miRNA (Xu et al., 2019a).
EIS or impedance biosensor is a detection method that can reflect biochemical processes and biometric events on the electrode surface by measurement of their capacitance and interfacial charge transfer resistance (Shamsuddin et al., 2021; Xue et al., 2020). The interfacial charges undergo modulations during the target/biorecognition probe formation. However, the detection limit is not desirable. The introduction of the enzymatic biocatalytic deposition (ECP) signal amplification technique is considered as an effective approach to improving the performance of the EIS biosensor (Hou et al., 2016; Sun et al., 2015). In the classical ECP
process, a layer of insoluble products was generated on the electrode with the aid of enzymes and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) (Hou et al., 2014; Zhuang et al., 2015), which can drastically enhance the detection signal. However, the biological activity of natural enzymes is easily affected by external conditions (temperature and pH value), and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) molecule is easy to decompose in air, etc., which limits the wide application of biosensors (Kou et al., 2016). Therefore, it is of great significance to find a new type of biocatalytic precipitator for catalytic oxidation of organic substrate 4-chloro-1-naphthol (4-CN) to form insoluble products without using \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\).
MOFs, a new class of hybrid material constructed from organic ligands as struts and their coordinated metal ions (or clusters) as nodes, have gained significantly increasing attention due to their intriguing superiority, such as easy synthesis and functionalization, large specific areas, high loading capacities, and versatile designability (Carrington et al., 2017; Cheng et al., 2016; Fu et al., 2017; Kim et al., 2017; Lustig et al., 2017; Wu and Yang, 2017). It has found wide applications in many fields, such as gas storage and separation (Chen et al., 2017a), chemical sensing (Chang et al., 2019), nonlinear optics (Jiang et al., 2018), luminescence (Andreo et al., 2018), catalysis (Wang and Astruc, 2020), and others. Moreover, one can easily and predictably tune the shape, cavity size, and functionality of MOFs through synthetic modification of the organic component, which shows huge potential and bright prospects for constructing biosensors. Li and his workers developed a simple electrochemical biosensor for ultrasensitive exosome assay by using Zr-MOFs and aptamer as the recognition system (Liu et al., 2021). Cai et al. developed a fluorescence-colorimetric dual signal biosensor by capsulation of gold nanoparticles with aggregation-induced emission effect into MOFs (Cai et al., 2021). However, to date, the EIS biosensors built based on MOFs with predictable designs are still in the initial stage. One major challenge for MOFs in the fabrication of EIS biosensors is the incorporation of binding sites for guest recognition and reactive site for catalytic reactions in MOFs.
Bimetallic Prussian blue analog (PBA), a special metal-organic framework (MOF) [expressed as \(A_{x}M/M^{\prime}(C N)_{6}\mathrm{y}_{\mathrm{~\rightmoon~}}\) : \(z H_{2}O\) (A: alkaline metal like \(N a^{+}\) or \(K^{+}\) ; \(M\) , \(M^{+}\) transition metals cations)], has drawn increasing attention due to its intrinsic advantages like diverse composition, abundant redox active sites and open coordination structure (Jiao et al., 2017; Mizuno et al., 2013). It has been widely reported for extensive application in the field of hydrogen evolution, alkali metal ions batteries, catalysis, energy storage, and biosensors (Gao et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2019). Besides, the diversity of external ions in the complexes endows PBA with diverse valence states and adjustable redox potentials. Recently, bimetallic Cu–Fe Prussian blue analog (CuFe PBA) have been designed as their distinguished enzyme mimetic activity, which shows potential for sensing application in colorimetric detection. Prakash's group constructed a colorimetric biosensor for quantification of Isoniazid by employing the intrinsic oxidase mimetic CuFe PBA nanocube (Ojha et al., 2021). Lai and his co-operators designed CuFe PBA with oxidase-mimicking activity for colorimetric biosensors for aflatoxin B1 (Lai et al., 2022). Thus, it seems like that Cu–Fe PBA may be applied as a powerful biocatalytic precipitator for catalytic oxidation of organic substrate 4-chloro-1-naphthol without \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\).
Signal amplification plays vital role in development of highly sensitive EIS biosensor. DNA walker, a class of developing DNA machines, has attracted increasing attention in designing and fabricating DNA based biosensors. It can perform repeated autonomous and progressive movement along the predeterminate DNA orbit, thus accumulate signal cascade amplification during the walking process. Multiple DNA walker amplified biosensor has been constructed for the determination of various analytes, including toxic molecules (Wang et al., 2021), biomarkers (Li et al., 2022), nucleic acids (Zhu et al., 2018), and metal ions (Qing et al., 2018), etc. Inspired by the research, \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) modified \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MOF nanostructures (Cu-NMOF) were designed and synthesized to develop a highly sensitive impedimetric biosensor for detection of miRNA-10b by combing the 3D DNA walker signal amplification in the paper. In the design, 2-aminoterephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\), which is rich in amino and carboxyl groups, was chosen as the organic ligands for preparing NMOF. In this way, DNA strands (complementary strand, CS) can be loaded on the Cu-NMOF for preparing CS modified NMOF@Cu2+ (CS-NMOF@Cu2+) through the direct condensation of carboxyl groups with amino groups, which can hybrid with the assistant strands that released from the target triggered 3D DNA walker system. Meanwhile, abundant copper ions can be assembled on the NMOF through the coordination reaction between \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), which served as the skeleton for the in-situ generation of CuFe PBA (CuFe PBA@NMOF). The formed CuFe PBA@NMOF can efficiently catalyze the oxidation of 4-CN to produce a layer of the precipitator. Thus, the electrochemical signal can be largely enhanced.
