## 2.7. Construction of electrochemical biosensor
Firstly, the supernatant collected by the movement of the DNA walker (10 \(\upmu\mathrm{L}\)) was dropped onto the Au/GCE and incubated for 2 h at 37 °C to immobilize the AS onto the Au/GCE surface. Straight after, a 10 \(\upmu\mathrm{L}\) CS-NMOF@Cu2+ probe was dropped onto the electrode surface and reacted for 1 h at room temperature. Following that, \(\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) (10 \(\upmu\mathrm{L}\), 20 mM) was dropped on the electrode surface and reacted for 6 min at room temperature. During this process, \(\left[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right]^{3-}\) can react with the \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\) on the probe to form CuFe PBA@NMOF. The electrode was then washed successively with ultrapure water. Afterward, 4-CN (5 mM) solution was added dropwise to the electrode and the reaction was carried out for 25 min at room temperature without light. In the process, the CuFe PBA@NMOF could fully catalyze the deposition of 4-CN. At last, the sediments adsorbed on the electrode surface were washed with PBS solution.
## 2.8. Electrochemical measurement
2.8. Electrochemical measurementThe electrochemical measurements in this experiment were performed with a model CHI760E Electrochemical Workstation (Shanghai, China). Throughout the experiment, a three-electrode system was used with a GCE working electrode, \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\) reference electrode, and a platinum wire as the auxiliary electrode. The potential range for cyclic voltammetry (CV) was \(-0.2\) to \(0.6\;\mathrm{V}\) at a scan rate of \(50\;\mathrm{mV/s}\). Meanwhile, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were taken at data acquisition frequency from \(0.1\;\mathrm{Hz}\) to \(10^{5}\;\mathrm{Hz}\) with an applied potential of \(230\;\mathrm{mV}\), using an alternating voltage of \(5\;\mathrm{mV}\).
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Principle of the electrochemical detection of miRNA-10b
3.1. Principle of the electrochemical detection of miRNA-10bInspired by the above study, this paper proposes a novel impedimetric biosensor for ultrasensitive detection of miRNA-10b based on a 3D DNA walker and instant catalyst with oxidase-mimicking activity. Initially, locking strands (LS) containing complementary sequences to miRNA-10b were hybridized with DNAzyme strands (DS) to form walking strands (WS). Subsequently, WS and substrate strands (SS) are anchored to MB to form a 3D DNA walker. In the presence of the miRNA-10b, the LS will be displaced by the strand displacement reaction and the DS will be released. Then, the liberated WS can hybridize with the SS. The SS gets cleaved by the unprotected DS by taking \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\) as a cofactor, and the assistant strands (AS) are released. This cleavage reaction provides the energy for DS to move from one SS to another, thus allowing it to walk autonomously along the MB, and more and more SS will be sheared, outputting a large amount of AS (Scheme 1b).Furthermore, the output AS was anchored to the modified electrode via an Au–S bond. A large number of \(\mathrm{CS-NMOF@Cu}^{2+}\) probes (Scheme 1a) can be assembled on the electrode surface through complementarily
Furthermore, the output AS was anchored to the modified electrode via an Au–S bond. A large number of \(\mathrm{CS-NMOF@Cu}^{2+}\) probes (Scheme 1a) can be assembled on the electrode surface through complementarily
hybridizing with AS. Afterward, plenty of CuFe PBA@NMOF was generated on the electrode surface in situ after a challenge with \(\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) solution. The generated CuFe PBA with oxidase-mimicking activity can oxide 4-CN to generate insoluble products in the absence of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and deposit them on the surface of the sensing platform, leading to the increase of electrochemical signal. Due to the powerful signal amplification ability of the DNA walker, the ultra-sensitive detection of miRNA-10b can be achieved indirectly by observing the increase in resistance after the reaction with 4-CN.
## 3.2. Characterization of the synthesized NMOF
3.2. Characterization of the synthesized NMOFFor our design, the successful synthesis of NMOF is essential for this biosensor. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were performed to analyze the surface chemical state of the synthesized NMOF. The XPS survey spectrum, as shown in Fig. 1A, verified the presence of C, Zr, N, and O in the synthesized NMOF. Two peaks at \(182.3\,\mathrm{eV}\) and \(184.7\,\mathrm{eV}\) in the spectra of Zr 3d (Fig. 1B) were attributed to Zr \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) and Zr \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\), respectively (Pe˜nas-Garz´on et al., 2022). The N 1s spectra of NMOF also displayed two peaks at \(399.1\;\mathrm{eV}\) and \(400.1\;\mathrm{eV}\) (Fig. 1C), which are associated with the amino group \(\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\) and the protonated amidogen form \(\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}\right)\), respectively (Pe˜nas-Garz´on et al., 2022). Additionally, the O 1s spectrum's deconvolution showed two peaks at 531.5 and \(531.9\;\mathrm{eV}\) that were attributed to \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) and Zr–O species, respectively (Pe˜nas-Garz´on et al., 2022) (Fig. 1D). The production of NMOF from the component and chemical states was confirmed by the XPS data.Subsequently, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was utilized to characterize the morphology of the synthesized NMOF. Fig. 2A clearly showed the octahedral crystal structure of NMOF with a smooth material surface, well-defined edges, and a particle size of about \(80\;\mathrm{nm}\), which is consistent with that described in the literature (Luan et al., 2014). As can be seen from Fig. 2B, the uniform octahedral structure
Subsequently, scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was utilized to characterize the morphology of the synthesized NMOF. Fig. 2A clearly showed the octahedral crystal structure of NMOF with a smooth material surface, well-defined edges, and a particle size of about \(80\ \mathrm{nm}\) , which is consistent with that described in the literature (Luan et al., 2014). As can be seen from Fig. 2B, the uniform octahedral structure
became rougher and the size of the particle was increased after the \(\mathrm{NMOF}@C u^{2+}\) reacted with \(\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) . It seems the CuFe PBA can be formed on the surface of \(\mathrm{{Cu}}^{2+}\) modified NMOF by the reaction between the \(\mathrm{{Cu}}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) .
To further confirm the elemental composition of the formed particles on NMOF, energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) characterization was applied in Fig. 2C. Compared to NMOF (black line), the characteristic peak of Cu was observed in \(\mathrm{NMOF}@C u^{2+}\) (red line), which preliminary demonstrated the successful modification of \(\mathrm{{Cu}}^{2+}\) on the NMOF. Furthermore, a new peak of Fe appeared in \(\mathrm{NMOF}@C u^{2+}\) after it was
challenged with \(\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) (blue line). In addition, X-ray diffraction (XRD) was applied for the analysis of the crystal structure of NMOF, as shown in Fig. 2D. The XRD pattern of NMOF (red line) showed the diffraction peaks at \(2\theta\) degree of \(7.34^{\circ}\) and \(8.48^{\circ}\) , which is in agreement with the simulated pattern in the literature (Penas-Garzón et al., 2022). Furthermore, there was no significant loss in the crystallinity (blue line) after the coordination of \(\mathrm{{Cu}}^{2+}\) with NMOF, indicating that the skeleton of NMOF was mostly maintained. Significantly, five new diffraction peaks at 2θ degrees of \(17.65^{\circ}\) , \(24.97^{\circ}\) , \(50.01^{\circ}\) , \(58.18^{\circ}\) , and \(66.31^{\circ}\) were generated after the \(\mathrm{NMOF}@C u^{2+}\) treatment with \(\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) (green
line), which attributed to (200), (220), (440), (620), and (640) lattice planes of CuFe PBA (JCPDS No. 70–2702). Obviously, the results indicate that CuFe PBA has been successfully generated in situ on the skeleton of NMOF. Moreover, Fourier transformed infrared (FT-IR) spectroscopy was further used to validate the formation of \(\mathrm{CS-NMOF@Cu}^{2+}\). As shown in Fig. S1, NMOF (black line) has a characteristic absorption peak at \(3341~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which corresponds to the O–H vibrational frequency of the COOH group (Prabhavathi et al., 2017). The amino \(\left(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right)\) bending vibration absorption peak was observed at 1652 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) at the same time (Hu et al., 2022). The aforementioned findings demonstrate that NMOF containing amino and carboxyl groups can load plentiful copper ions and cross-link CS, respectively. Subsequently, the characteristic absorption peak at \(1652\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) was significantly weakened when the copper ion was coordinated with \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) (red line), which confirmed the successful coordination of copper ion with NMOF to form \(\mathrm{NMOF@Cu}^{2+}\). Inspiringly, a new absorption peak at \(1628\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ascribed to amide bond was appeared in \(\mathrm{CS-NMOF@Cu}^{2+}\) (blue line) due to the amidation reaction between \(\mathrm{NMOF@Cu}^{2+}\) and CS (Bao et al., 2020; Shrivastava et al., 2016). The above results can infer the successful formation of \(\mathrm{CS-NMOF@Cu}^{2+}\).
## 3.3. Feasibility investigation of the 3D DNA walker
The 3D DNA walker plays a key role in identifying target miRNA and transducing the amplified detection signal. Therefore, \(15\%\) polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (PAGE) was employed to confirm that the 3D DNA walker had been successfully constructed. As shown in Fig. S2, lanes 1–8 represented the bands for SS, LS, DS, the mixture of DS and LS (WS), the mixture of LS and miRNA-10b (LS/miRNA-10b), the mixture of WS and miRNA-10b, the mixture of WS, SS, and \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\), the mixture of WS, SS, miRNA-10b, and \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\), respectively. It was observed that LS can hybridize with miRNA-10b, producing a band of large DNA fragments (lane 5). When there is no miRNA-10b, the band corresponding to the SS was observed (lane 7). This indicates that DS is locked by the LS and the cleavage reaction cannot be triggered. When miRNA-10b was present, the band of SS vanished (lane 8). Meanwhile, a band corresponding to
the hybridization of LS with miRNA-10b and a band of DS appeared in lane 6, indicating that miRNA-10b can hybridize with LS to form a duplex (LS/miRNA-10b) via a strand displacement reaction, accompanied by the release of DS. The result demonstrated that the SS can be cleaved by the DNAzyme sequence with the aid of \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\), meanwhile, the 3D DNA walker can be initiated. Thus, the developed 3D DNA walker can be feasibly used to identify miRNA-10b as well as amplify the detection signal.
## 3.4. EIS characterization of the biosensor and comparison of various signal-generation tags
Based on the oxidase-mimicking activity of the instant catalyst CuFe PBA@NMOF, as well as the signal amplification of the DNA walker, we develop an impedance biosensing platform for detecting miRNA-10b. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopic (EIS) was utilized to characterize the modification process on the electrode surface (displayed in Fig. 3A). When the AS released from the 3D DNA walker system was immobilized on Au/GCE via Au–S bonds, the resistance increased (curve b) compared to the gold nanoparticle modified GCE (curve a). The reason might be attributed to the weak conductivity of the DNA oligonucleotide chain. Then, the resistance was further increased after the \(\mathrm{CS-NMOF@Cu}^{2+}\) probe was captured on the AS/Au/GCE electrode and reacted with \(\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) (curve c). Meanwhile, the corresponding SEM images of the electrochemical surface during the modification process was added in Fig. S3. The results demonstrated the successful formation of CuFe PBA@NMOF on the electrode. Inspiringly, after being challenged with 4-CN, the resistance of the electrode was significantly increased. It seems like that CuFe PBA could effectively catalyze 4-CN to generate insoluble precipitates without \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) on the electrode surface, thus resulting in a large steric hindrance and blocking the electron transport.
In addition, another question worth noting was whether the deposition of 4-CN was catalyzed by the generated CuFe PBA or other components. To confirm this point, a control test was made. Three signal-generation tags (i.e. CS-NMOF, CS-NMOF@Cu2+, and CS-CuFe
PBA@NMOF)} were used for the detection of \(250\,\mathrm{pM}\) miRNA-10b on the same-batch AS modified Au/GCE with the same assay protocol. As shown in Fig. 3B and C, the CS-NMOF, and CS-NMOF@Cu\(^{2+}\) modified electrodes show almost no increase in resistance after reaction with 4-CN. In contrast, the CS-CuFe PBA@NMOF modified electrode shows a dramatic increase in resistance of \(69.72\%\) after the reaction with 4-CN (Fig. 3D). Meanwhile, SEM and Atomic force microscopy (AFM) were used to investigate the topology of CS-CuFe PBA@NMOF modified electrode before and after reaction with 4-CN. Compared with Fig. 3E, the electrode platform that employed 4-CN deposition was almost covered by a layer of polymer, indicating that insoluble precipitation is generated on the surface of the electrode (Fig. 3F). The results collected by AFM were consistent with the SEM (Fig. S4). The results further indicated that the amplification of the impedance signal is mainly derived from the nanocatalytic precipitation process facilitated by the in situ generated CuFe PBA.
## 3.5. Analytical performance of the electrochemical impedance biosensor
In order to achieve the optimal analytical performance of this biosensor for miRNA-10b, several parameters were optimized, such as the molar concentration ratio of SS and WS (15:1), the concentration of \(\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}(1.5\;\mathrm{mM})\), the incubation time of the DNA walker \((150\ \mathrm{min})\), the formation time of CuFe PBA \((6\,\mathrm{min})\), pH of base solution \((\mathrm{pH}\,7.4)\), and the incubation time of AS with Au/GCE (2 h) (Fig. S6, SM). Under optimal experimental conditions, the proposed electrochemical impedance biosensor based on CuFe PBA catalyzed 4-CN deposition and 3D DNA walker amplification strategy was employed to detect different concentrations of miRNA-10b standard samples. As depicted in Fig. 4A, the resistance increased with the increase of miRNA-10b concentration. There was a well-defined linear relationship between \(\Delta\mathbf{R}\) (Note: \(\Delta\mathbf{R}\) is the change in resistance before and after the precipitation of 4-CN) and the miRNA-10b concentration in the range of \(0.8\,\mathrm{pM}\) -250 pM (Fig. 4B) with a linear equation \(\Delta\mathrm{R}=8.79\,\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{miRNA-10b}}+1474.16\) \((\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9969)\). Additionally, the calculated limit of detection (LOD) was \(0.5\,\mathrm{pM}\,(3\sigma/\mathrm{s})\). Moreover, the proposed impedance biosensor exhibited expected sensitivity and LOD in detecting miRNA-10b compared to previously reported miRNA detection methods, as listed in Table S2.
## 3.6. Selectivity, reproducibility, and stability of the biosensor
In order to investigate the selectivity of this electrochemical impedance biosensor for miRNA-10b, the biosensor was tested with miRNA-21, miRNA-141, and miRNA-153 as potentially interfering substances. As shown in Fig. 5A, when other miRNAs were present, the resistance value was almost the same as that of the blank sample even though the concentration of these interferents was 100 times higher than that of the target. However, when miRNA-10b was present, the resistance was significantly increased. The experimental results indicated
that the biosensor can be used for the selective detection of miRNA-10b. In addition, the reproducibility of the prepared electrochemical impedance biosensor was also investigated in this experiment. Five different electrodes were used to prepare the sensing platform for the detection of miRNA-10b \((\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{miRNA-10b}}=200\ \mathrm{pM})\) under the same experimental conditions. The resistance values of the above five sensing platforms for miRNA-10b are shown in Fig. 5B, and the relative standard deviation (RSD) of the electrochemical signal was \(4.53\%\), indicating that the developed biosensor has superior reproducibility. Finally, the stability of the electrochemical impedance biosensor was explored. The prepared biosensor was stored at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for different weeks and then applied to detect miRNA-10b. The resistance change value decreased overall with increasing weeks of storage, but the response value of the biosensor could still account for \(92.3\%\), the initial value after 4 weeks of storage (Fig. 5C), implying that this electrochemical impedance biosensor has outstanding stability.
## 3.7. Detection of miRNA-10b in real samples
The content of miRNA-10b in serum samples of healthy people was measured by the standard recovery method to verify the feasibility and trueness of this electrochemical impedance biosensor. Before the experiment, human serum samples were pretreated with NaCl solution at a mass concentration of \(0.9\%\). Then, four different concentrations of standard miRNA-10b (0.8, 50, 100, and \(150\,\mathrm{pM}\), respectively) were added to diluted serum samples and used in 3D DNA walker signal amplification to construct the final electrochemical biosensor. According to the resistance response value and the linear equation above, the recovery concentration of miRNA-10b in serum can be obtained through calculation. As displayed in Table S3, the recovery rate was \(95.7\%\) - \(109.18\%\) and RSD less than \(3.72\%\). These results revealed that the electrochemical impedance biosensor could be used for detecting target miRNA-10b in real samples.
## 4. Conclusions
In conclusion, our work effectively created a facile impedance biosensing platform based on \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-MOFs modified with \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), with the help of a 3D DNA walker signal amplification strategy for the sensitive detection of miRNA-10b. For use in biorecognition procedures, CS-NMOF@Cu\(^{2+}\) probes can be prepared by decorated carboxyl-rich organic ligands in \(\mathrm{NMOF@Cu}^{2+}\) with CS. After the target was added, the DNA walker was activated, shearing out a significant amount of AS, which was then immobilized on the GCE. Further, the CS-NMOF@Cu\(^{2+}\) probe can be attached to the electrode through the hybridization reaction with AS, which is applied as the skeleton for just in time generation of CuFe PBA@NMOF after reacting with \(K_{2}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\). Experiment results demonstrated that CuFe PBA with oxidative activity can catalyze the precipitation of 4-CN, enhancing the resistance of the sensing
platform.
Although the developed biosensor is time-consuming, it shows highly sensitive, excellent specificity and stability. In light of these advantages, the proposed impedance biosensor achieved the detection limit of \(0.5\,\mathrm{pM}\) and the detection range from \(0.8\,\mathrm{pM}\) to \(250\,\mathrm{pM}\), which is beneficial for both biosensing research and clinical assays.
